Excellent, you've successfully installed Git and your terminal can now find it! This is a different, and much simpler, problem to solve.

The error message "Permission denied" means that while the git command is working, your Windows operating system is blocking it from creating the necessary hidden .git folder inside your D:\dryv_app directory.

This is a very common security feature in Windows, especially when working directly in the root of a drive.

The Easiest Solution: Run as an Administrator
The simplest way to fix this is to give your terminal the necessary permissions by running it as an administrator.

Close Visual Studio Code completely.

Find the Visual Studio Code icon on your taskbar, Start Menu, or desktop.

Right-click on the icon.

From the context menu, select "Run as administrator".

Windows will ask for your permission; click "Yes".

Once VS Code opens with administrator rights, open your terminal and run the git init command again.

It should now work without any permission errors.



