Of course! Now that your app is built and working, let's go through the final steps to save it to GitHub using the latest code.

Here is the complete, step-by-step process.

Step 1: Create a Repository on GitHub
Go to GitHub and log in.

Create a new repository.

For the "Repository name", use the format flutter-upskill-capstone-[your surname] (e.g., flutter-upskill-capstone-jayag).

Make sure the repository is Public.

Do not check any of the boxes to add a README, .gitignore, or license. Keep it empty.

Click the "Create repository" button.

On the next page, you will see a URL. Click the copy button next to it. This is your repository's URL.

Step 2: Prepare Your Local Project for Git
Before we push the code, we need to tell Git which files to ignore. This is a very important step for Flutter projects.

In VS Code, inside your project's root directory (D:\dryv_app), create a new file and name it exactly .gitignore (starting with a dot).

Copy all the text from the code block below and paste it into your new .gitignore file.

Code snippet

